  {
   "id": "concept2",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Differentiator and comparator"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "In the differentiator the positions are swapped: C at the input and R in the feedback. The capacitor current $C\\,dV_i/dt$ flows through R, so $V_o = -RC\\,dV_i/dt$. A triangular wave becomes a square wave and a sine becomes a (negative) cosine whose amplitude grows with frequency, $2\\pi f RC V_m$. A comparator uses the op-amp without feedback: because the gain is huge, the output goes to $+V_{sat}$ when $V_+ > V_-$ and to $-V_{sat}$ when $V_+ < V_-$. With one input grounded it is a zero-crossing detector that turns a sine wave into a square wave."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "Differentiator: Vo = −RC dVi/dt"
    },
    {
     "en": "Triangle → square; sine → cosine"
    },
    {
     "en": "Comparator: no feedback, output ±Vsat"
    },
    {
     "en": "Zero-crossing detector: sine → square"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "The differentiator has C at the input and R in the feedback, so its output is minus RC times the rate of change of the input. A comparator has no feedback and swings to plus or minus saturation.",
    "kn": "ಡಿಫರೆನ್ಷಿಯೇಟರ್‌ನಲ್ಲಿ ಇನ್‌ಪುಟ್‌ನಲ್ಲಿ C, ಪ್ರತಿಪೋಷಣೆಯಲ್ಲಿ R: Vo = −RC dVi/dt. ಕಂಪೇರೇಟರ್‌ಗೆ ಪ್ರತಿಪೋಷಣೆ ಇಲ್ಲ; ಔಟ್‌ಪುಟ್ +Vsat ಅಥವಾ −Vsat."
   },
   "formula": "V_o = -RC\\,\\frac{dV_i}{dt}"
  },
